    "topic": "The first rubber eraser was an accident",
    "summary": "Before rubber erasers, tablets of wax and crustless bread were used to erase pencil marks. In 1770 Joseph Priestley described a substance excellently adapted to wiping black-lead-pencil marks from paper. Edward Nairne, an English optician and instrument maker, reportedly developed the first widely marketed rubber eraser; according to Nairne, he picked up a piece of rubber instead of breadcrumbs. In 1770 'rubber' meant any object used for rubbing; the word attached to the new material between 1770 and 1778. Vulcanisation (1839) made rubber durable. In 1858 Hymen Lipman patented attaching an eraser to a pencil; the patent was later invalidated.",
    "angle": "A happy accident that named a material.",
